WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.990
Dany: Do you worry that a lot of the work
that was done after 9/11 is coming undone?

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:11.830
Cheney: I worry that a lot of it's been set
aside by the Obama Administration and now

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:17.089
15 years later, it's become fashionable in
some circles, including sometimes on the Hill

00:00:17.089 --> 00:00:26.839
with some of my own Republican friends, that
we've forgotten or choose to set aside the

00:00:26.839 --> 00:00:33.670
events of 9/11. When you have 19 guys armed
with airline tickets and box cutters come

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.370
to the United States, hijack aircraft, kill
3,000 of our people, take down the World Trade

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.039
Center, blow a hole in the Pentagon, would
have taken out probably the White House or

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:49.329
the Capitol Building if it hadn't been for
the passengers on United 93, that's a history-altering

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:53.809
event. It's an act of war. It's worse than
Pearl Harbor.

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:58.960
And yet here we are 15 years later and people
are sort of thinking, "Well, that was a one-off

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.530
affair," or we can afford to get rid of the
database we built with the terror surveillance

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:12.460
program, turn it all over to the companies,
and ease off on the policies we put in place

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:18.159
that I believe prevented further attacks.
And it's unfortunate. It's as though we're

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:22.340
going to have to get to the point where we
have another attack before everybody's ready

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.590
to really buckle down again and do those things
that are necessary to keep us safe.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:31.610
Dany: So one of the things that was said at
the end of the Bush Administration was the

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.600
policies that you put in place are succeeding
and the evidence of that is there hasn't been

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.240
another attack.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:36.880
Cheney: Right.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.399
Dany: And there hasn't been certainly another,
there have been attempts in the United States.

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:41.579
Cheney: Right.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:46.820
Dany: There have also been some lone wolf
or self-starter terrorist attacks, but there

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:52.649
hasn't actually been an attack. Is that a
testimony to the capabilities of the intelligence

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:57.490
community, a testimony to the incapabilities
of the terrorist group? How do you see that?

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.939
Because I think that might be what President
Obama would say. "What's your evidence?"

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:07.159
Cheney: Yeah. Well, we had a report even,
for example, right after 9/11 that Al Qaeda

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:07.899
was in Pakistan. Excuse me. We had evidence
right after 9/11 that Al Qaeda was seeking

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:14.110
a nuclear device in Pakistan, where they have
nuclear devices. We had to be concerned from

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:20.080
the very beginning about those kinds of things
and we had reporting about attacks that never

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:29.370
came off, attack on the highest building on
the West Coast of the United States, attack

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.019
in downtown London.

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:36.659
So it's clear that what we did, the terrorist
surveillance program and so-forth, did put

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:44.069
in place the necessary steps. The fact of
that we didn't get hit again I really think

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:49.590
has a lot to do with the vigilance, with the
airline security measures we put in place,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.849
we had attacks fail such as the underwear
bomber over Detroit. So there's no doubt in

00:02:54.849 --> 00:03:00.080
my mind it was a positive, successful policy.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:12.340
And so the notion that Barack Obama brought
to office was that first of all, that we'd

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:17.129
over-stepped the bounds. Remember when he
went to Cairo and he apologized to the Muslim

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:26.349
world for our over-reaction to 9/11. One thing
that I found out later was that if you look

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:32.379
at some of the documents that were released
when Snowden stole documents, that we uncovered

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:38.360
when Liz and I were doing our latest book
was that our ambassador to Japan had sent

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:43.549
a cable back to the White House during that
first year when Obama was doing his apology

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:48.780
tour saying the Japanese had rejected his
request that he be allowed to go to Hiroshima

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:54.599
and apologize for our having dropped the bomb
on Hiroshima to end World War II.

00:03:54.599 --> 00:04:02.170
So he set a tone out there that...and a set
of policies, including what he's done to defense...that

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.390
have persuaded a lot of our friends and allies
around the world that they can no longer count

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:16.580
on the United States. That we don't have their
back. And I think it shows in terms of the

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:21.720
relationships we have with so many of those
folks that the United States is no longer

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.820
looked to as the go-to people to deal with
the terrorist problem.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.080
Dany: I want to come back to the question
of defense quickly in a minute, but let me

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:32.380
ask you about the Middle East. It is not the
place it was in 2008 when you and President

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:40.250
Bush left office. There are conflicts from
Yemen to Syria to Lebanon to Iraq to Libya,

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.580
throughout North Africa. Al Qaeda is really
back and many suggest it's as strong as it

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:53.060
was on September 10th, 2001. What do you see
as the root of those conflicts? Do you think

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.470
the United States bears any responsibility
for that?

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:04.840
Cheney: I don't. I think some people have
suggested our actions in effect promoted or

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:12.440
generated 9/11. That's hogwash. We were not
doing anything that merited that attack. It

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.900
was the terrorists themselves who decided
they wanted to hit the United States. The

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.850
Mideast has been a place for conflict for
a long time. When I was Secretary of Defense,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.760
Saddam Hussein invaded Kuwait and we had to
do Desert Storm. But at the time, we were

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:32.540
able to organize the entire region in order
to prevail, if you will. It was a great coalition

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:37.550
put together. We had a $60 billion war that
cost us 5 billion because others chipped in.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.920
It was a unique moment when you pull all of
that together and when the U.S. was clearly

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:44.710
preeminent in the region.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:50.070
Since then, over time obviously there have
been the rise of ISIS, the development of

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:59.610
efforts to establish a caliphate, which they've
successfully done now in Syria and Iraq. We've

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:05.270
seen efforts by the Iranians to dominate.
They now are sort of preeminent if you will

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:16.260
in Beirut, in Damascus, in Yemen. The IRGC
has been very, very active over the years.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:22.580
The Iranians have been very active over the
years pursuing the title they bear as the

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.270
world's worst state sponsor of terror. And
all of that's been going on now for a long

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.960
time before we got involved.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:33.210
Dany: So one argument I often have with people
in Washington is about whether the Iraq war

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.740
was a triggering factor. There's a suggestion
that a lot of people like to make...and it's

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:44.280
not really a partisan suggestion, more of
an ideological one...that getting rid of Saddam

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.230
Hussein just sort of ripped the lid off the
Middle East and it was from thence that everything

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:55.130
began to domino, topple regimes, the Arab
Spring. What do you say to that?

00:06:55.130 --> 00:07:00.440
Cheney: Well, I say you've got to look back
at history. We had a very serious nuclear

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:07.660
proliferation problem in the Middle East that
goes back to 1981. Long before we did anything

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:13.990
vis-à-vis Saddam Hussein but in '81, he had
a nuclear program, the Osirak reactor outside

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:18.150
Baghdad. The Israelis took it out. When I
was secretary of defense in the summer of

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:24.050
'90, I was visited by my Israeli counterpart,
the Defense Minister, Moshe Arens, and the

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:30.240
Senior Uniformed Officer, Ehud Barak, and
they showed me evidence that Saddam had started

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:36.490
up in 1990 a nuclear program again and that
it was pretty far along.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:43.660
In Desert Storm, we went in, we took out that
program. The IAEA went in afterwards and did

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:47.960
an investigation and found out that he was
much closer than we thought to actually having

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:55.270
nuclear capability. This was an independent,
international atomic energy agency that went

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:58.490
in and checked that. That's not something
the United States came up with and that was

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:05.130
before we ever got into the WMD debate with
respect to when we went in in 2003. It's a

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:11.870
long history of Saddam Hussein trying to develop
weapons of mass destruction. The first intelligence

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:18.620
report I saw after I got elected vice president
in 2000 warned about Iraq and WMD.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:31.440
So the activities of Saddam Hussein overwhelmingly
created ferment and turmoil and raised the

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.710
specter, especially the link between terror
on the one hand and weapons of mass destruction

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:40.430
on the other. He used chemical weapons against
thousands of his own people at Halabja in

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:46.330
1988. I mean, he had a well-established track
record that he was a troublemaker and he had

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:54.190
invaded Kuwait before and we did go in with
the support of a lot of Democrats in '03 and

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:59.400
we achieved our objective in that we got rid
of Saddam Hussein.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:05.700
That also precipitated other activities. We
found out, for example, that Muammar Qaddafi

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:11.020
had a nuclear program and five days after
we captured Saddam Hussein, he surrendered

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:17.830
all of his nuclear materials to us. His centrifuges,
uranium feed stock, weapons design.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:24.070
So the circumstances over there in terms of
the problems and the difficulties that were

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:30.200
developing pointed in the direction of what
was my biggest fear, which was the possibility

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:36.810
that terrorists would ultimately acquire something
far deadlier than airline tickets and box

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:41.450
cutters and would acquire a nuclear weapon
or chemicals or a biological agent. I was

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:47.080
quoted in an interview I did about five months
before 9/11 that that was out biggest threat

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:51.840
and I think it still is today. And we've still
got a very serious nuclear proliferation problem

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.340
now that goes back over 30 years.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.690
Dany: Right. And the Iranians of course are
unleashed at this point.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:01.660
Cheney: The Iranians are unleashed. I don't want to spend all your time on

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:11.500
the subject, but we had in 1987, Mr. A.Q.
Khan, who put together the Pakistani program

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:17.600
in a hotel in Dubai, sold the Iranians the
basic plans for centrifuges for $3 million.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:18.230
Nineteen eighty-seven.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:19.280
Dany: Right.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:24.220
Cheney: We found, of course, on our watch
when Meir Dagan showed up, the Israeli head

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:29.680
of Mossad, that Syria had a nuclear reactor
that had been built for them by the North

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:35.440
Koreans. The Israelis took it out in the fall
of '07. What's worked in that part of the

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:39.240
world for dealing with the nuclear problem
has been military force or the threat of military

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:46.430
force. And the thing about the Obama Administration
is they didn't pursue that option. Obama wrote

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:50.700
it off from the very beginning. What they
did was try to bribe the Iranians into being

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:55.330
good guys and giving up their program, but
the net result of that was to provide them

00:10:55.330 --> 00:11:00.910
with $150 billion of money that they can spend
on supporting terror.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:05.340
We have altered our policies I think during
the Obama Administration in major ways to

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:11.590
appease the Iranians, when strength and the
possible use of force has been the key in

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:16.400
the past. But it hasn't been used in this
administration at all and I think that's to

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:17.770
the detriment of our interest.

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:23.910
Dany: The perception in the region certainly
is that we are looking at the region through

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:31.530
the prism of Tehran. I agree with you about
that. There are candidates for president and

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:37.090
members of Congress, Republicans and Democrats,
who have suggested that the Middle East was

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:41.490
better off and that the world was better off
with not just Saddam Hussein in power but

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:46.230
Saddam Hussein and Bashar al-Assad and the
strong men who really characterized the Middle

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:48.340
East for the last half-century.

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:48.880
Cheney: Right.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.779
Dany: What do you say to that?

00:11:50.779 --> 00:11:56.630
Cheney: Well, that may make sense 50 years
ago. You wanted to get behind your oceans

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:03.550
and be safe and sound and comfortable. But
in my mind all of that ended when terrorist

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:08.590
organizations were able to use those territories
as a base of operation to train and equip

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:10.270
the attack of 9/11.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:16.360
When 19 guys can show up over here with airline
tickets and box cutters and kill 3,000 of

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:26.660
our people and blow up the World Trade Center,
the idea that you can let that set of whatever

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:30.850
you want to call them, dictators...point is
that Afghanistan for example had become a

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:38.300
safe harbor for Al Qaeda. And Syria now is
a safe harbor for ISIS. We know they're trying

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:42.430
to attack us, they want to hit us in the homeland.
They've already launched attacks that have

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:47.100
threatened us and our friends and allies.
Bombing the buses in London, for example,

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:52.920
or what happened in France a short time ago.
And they've done it here in the United States.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:13:01.500
And so the notion that we can allow somebody
like Saddam Hussein or a situation that's

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:08.570
happened in Libya, that it's perfectly okay
what they're doing, they can't reach us, they

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:15.330
can't hurt us. And that's just not true. Muammar
Qaddafi obviously got religion when we took

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:21.420
down Saddam Hussein. If we hadn't done that,
if he'd been allowed to develop his nuclear

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:27.070
program, then he was overthrown by ISIS, then
the debate would be, well, who's got the nukes?

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:29.480
Has ISIS inherited them from the old regime?

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:34.010
Same thing in Syria. If the Israelis hadn't
taken out that North Korean-built reactor

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:40.230
in Syria, then today that area, Al-Khabar
in Eastern Syria occupied by ISIS, they would

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:45.770
also now be the proud owners of the nuclear
reactor. So developments on the ground over

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:50.800
there are I think ultimately a direct threat
to the United States, to the safety and survival

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:57.880
of the United States. The technology has developed.
We are vulnerable to those organizations as

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:03.110
long as they have access to safe harbors and
sanctuary, as long as they have the ability

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.210
to acquire deadlier weapons.

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:11.730
Pakistan has a nuclear program, has had for
years, but out of Pakistan emerged A.Q. Khan.

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:17.220
He set up his own black market operation.
Libya was his biggest customer after we got

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:20.500
Qaddafi's materials, then we went and took
down A.Q. Khan. He's under house arrest, has

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:24.620
been in Pakistan. But he has testified in
the newspapers, in a newspaper story to the

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:29.740
effect that the North Koreans now have state-of-the-art
centrifuge technology. Where'd they get it?

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:31.380
They got it from Pakistan.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:37.620
So you've already got sort of the seed of
very destructive capabilities that could be

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.450
used against the United States or our friends
and allies. It's there. It's in the Middle

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:47.410
East. There was one report that the Iranians
paid for the last ballistic missile test by

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.080
the North Koreans.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:55.410
So the ability for us to say, "Well, it's
okay if Saddam stays in power..." He's going

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:59.460
to mess with weapons of mass destruction and
he had before. You can argue about how much

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:03.520
was there when we went in in '03 but there's
no question he had been involved previously,

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:09.089
that he's had them and used them. Or the Libyan
program or the Iranian program or the Pakistani

00:15:09.089 --> 00:15:10.250
program.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:17.110
It's a place we have to be involved. We're
far beyond the point when you could look at

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.930
it as a remote part of the world and it's
all right if there's some dictator over there

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:23.790
mistreating his people. It's about our safety
and survival as a nation.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:25.270
 

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:25.740
 

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:25.830
 

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:31.380
Dany: Was it a mistake for the United States
and NATO to help take out Muammar Qaddafi?

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:36.570
Cheney: I wasn't involved at that point. I'd
left the government and so I don't know everything

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:42.510
there is to know about it. Qaddafi had, to
some extent, come around in the sense that

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:50.149
he had surrendered his nuclear materials to
us. And I, just as not having been part of

00:15:50.149 --> 00:15:55.470
the process, I'm reluctant to pass judgment
on it. I think the follow-through obviously

00:15:55.470 --> 00:16:05.089
has been the destruction of Qaddafi and his
regime and ISIS now has a significant presence

00:16:05.089 --> 00:16:11.710
in Libya and that's right across the Med from
Italy and Europe. But not having been part

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.410
of the debate internally I just...I can't
make that judgment.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:18.620
Dany: I have to comment too that it is ironic
to me that a government that rose to power

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:23.610
on the back of criticism of the Iraq war and
how the aftermath was carried out, walked

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:27.050
away from Libya as if there were no lessons
from the Iraq war.

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:27.779
Cheney: Right.

00:16:27.779 --> 00:16:35.870
Dany: It is fascinating. So just to...Russia
is now in Syria. Vladimir Putin has taken

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:39.850
the Crimea. We seem to have forgotten about
it as a nation already. They're in Donetsk,

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:45.020
they're in Luhansk. There's some suspicion
they may even go into some of our NATO-allied

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:47.710
countries. Are we handling Putin correctly?

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:53.110
Cheney: I don't think so. I think Putin believes
he's got a window here that runs to the end

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:59.190
of the Obama Administration. We've seen him
move on to Crimea as you pointed out, in the

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:05.160
Ukraine, now he's in Syria. It's as though
he is trying to recreate elements of the old

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:10.230
Soviet Empire that came apart back in '90
and '91.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:14.920
I think he also has aspirations on the Baltics.
I think he'd like nothing better than to be

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:21.970
able to put pressure on the Baltics. Not so
much directly by military action, but they

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:27.260
all get all of their energy supplies, their
gas all comes from Russia. They were all part

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:31.580
of Russia at one time. The Russians appear
to be spending money on trying to influence

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:34.250
the internal politics of the Baltics.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:39.350
So if he could stir up enough trouble, I could
see him trying to put pressure on the Baltics

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:45.120
that they either have to do what he wants
them to do, probably withdraw from NATO, or

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:49.900
some additional pressures will be brought
to bear on the Baltics. I think that's his

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:56.510
real objective. Now will he pull it off? I
don't know and I question at this point how

00:17:56.510 --> 00:18:02.870
determined NATO would in fact be without strong
U.S. leadership. We provide 75% of the funding

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:08.640
for NATO and the defense budgets here in the
U.S. have been cut dramatically. That's provided

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:13.350
a rationale for the Europeans to go forward and
cut their defense budgets as well. So it's

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:13.450
worrisome.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.290
Dany: It's not just the Democrats who cut defense

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:16.630
budgets.

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:17.300
Cheney: Right.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:22.130
Dany: Republicans and Democrats came together.
We've lost a trillion dollars over 10 years

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:26.040
in defense spending. Why?

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:34.230
Cheney: Well, because Congress couldn't get
its act together back in, what was it, 2011.

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:40.630
Passed the Budget Control Act in 2011. But
that basically imposed and set up a mechanism

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:48.500
through sequestration that in effect says
if Congress doesn't get budgets passed and

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:52.809
adopted, then the meat axe falls. The sequester
takes place.

00:18:52.809 --> 00:18:56.679
And what's happened now is instead of the
traditional way of doing business in the Defense

00:18:56.679 --> 00:19:00.450
Department...where you look at the world,
identify threats, figure out what we need

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:06.740
in order to counter those threats or to cope
with them and then ask for the amount of money

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:11.080
that's involved...we don't do that anymore.
Now what happens is the inability to come

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:16.520
to an agreement has led to the frequent use
of the sequester.

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:21.190
The Defense Department makes up about 17%
of the budget. It takes 50% of the cuts because

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:27.050
a lot of the domestic programs have been exempted
from sequester. So the bottom line is that

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:32.190
Congress...and some of it on a partisan basis,
some of it on a bipartisan basis, there is

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:37.980
no question that there was Republican support
for this particular solution...but what it's

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:42.179
done over time is, this is four or five years
now since we put defense budgets together

00:19:42.179 --> 00:19:47.740
the way they ought to be put together, in
relation to the threat. We're now in a situation

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.450
where our capabilities are significantly diminished.

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:57.170
Ray Odierno, when he stepped down as the Army
chief last fall, said that the United States

00:19:57.170 --> 00:20:02.700
Army is less ready today than any time in
its history. That goes back over 200 years.

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:10.500
It's a very, very dangerous situation in the
sense that we've conveyed to our friends that

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:17.679
we don't want to act on their behalf. We conveyed
to others, including some of our adversaries,

00:20:17.679 --> 00:20:22.250
that we don't have the capability to act effectively.
We've changed.

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:27.400
If you look at the strategic concept that
always drove our planning in the past while

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:32.090
I was there...of course that was a long time
ago...but that we believed we needed to have

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:37.809
the capacity to fight two wars simultaneously
and enough left over to deal with other things

00:20:37.809 --> 00:20:44.460
on the side. That's gone. Our forces are so
much lower, so much less ready.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:49.940
Other things that are problems, we're not
investing as much as we need to in order to

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:53.640
counter the capabilities that are developed
by the Chinese and the Russians. The Russians

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:59.150
are modernizing their strategic forces. The
Chinese are as well. The Chinese have developed

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:03.299
the capability to take down satellites. They've
got a ballistic missile that will take out

00:21:03.299 --> 00:21:12.740
an aircraft carrier with a conventional warhead.
They've taken the Sai Islands in the South

00:21:12.740 --> 00:21:20.360
China Sea, built military bases there. The
Russians now are flying provocative missions

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:28.220
over parts of NATO territory and along borders
with NATO.

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:32.670
There's a major shift in the sense, in terms
of how they look at the United States. They

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:41.030
don't fear us any more. And I think that leads
them probably to underestimate or perhaps

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:47.470
overestimate how far they can go, but underestimate
what's sensitive for us. The world was very

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:52.730
safe and stable when the U.S. was the principal
power, when we had the military capability

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:58.980
to deal with any contingency and we were prepared
to use it when we felt the need. I don't think

00:21:58.980 --> 00:21:59.940
that's the case anymore.

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:06.400
Another point that's crucial is our military
advantage for a long time really since the

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:10.730
end of World War II has been based on our
technological edge. We were the first ones

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:15.580
with stealth, we were the first ones with
precision guided munitions, we had...remember

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:20.750
Desert Storm and the tremendous capabilities
we demonstrated. Nobody could match that.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:21.420
That day's passed.

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:27.470
The Chinese are moving aggressively to close
some of those gaps. The Russians are, too.

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:31.670
Or find ways around it so they come up with
countermeasures that are effective against

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:38.010
our armed forces. The whole area of cyber-warfare's
enormously important. And I worry that we're

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:42.820
not doing enough there and the Chinese have
gotten very good at it. So have the Russians.

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:51.730
Perhaps Al Qaeda or ISIS. And that capacity
to hack into our system. They've demonstrated

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:56.510
it by taking the personnel records of those
of us who worked for the federal government.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:58.030
I mean, it's not personal.

00:22:58.030 --> 00:22:58.640
Dany: Right.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:03.130
Cheney: But they've done it. And when you
think about the vulnerability of our system,

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:07.990
our reliance on technology, the extent to
which they could hack into our power grid

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:15.940
or other key elements of our infrastructure
is very real. And we are not doing what we

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:21.670
need to be doing because of budget cuts in
order to maintain our advantage and guarantee

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:22.559
our safety.

00:23:22.559 --> 00:23:28.840
Dany: You just in 30 seconds laid out an enormously
compelling case that the United States needs

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:34.030
to show leadership, that the United States
needs to reinvest, not for offense but for

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:41.380
deterrence. What's happened to Congress? What's
happened to the Republican Party?

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:49.610
Cheney: Well, I don't want to blame everybody
in the Republican Party obviously. A lot of

00:23:49.610 --> 00:23:53.240
it is you haven't had any presidential leadership
in this area. He's been going the other way.

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:59.080
He loves the sequester that frees up resources
that he can use for his domestic programs.

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:05.309
As I say, a lot of the domestic programs have
been exempted from the sequester.

00:24:05.309 --> 00:24:12.410
The whole process of putting together budgets
has broken down and I think one of the keys

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:19.059
for us has to be...and I've argued this with
my friends periodically, they ask me to come

00:24:19.059 --> 00:24:25.970
up and chat on different matters, which I'm
always happy to do...but we have to recognize

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:31.110
that our number one priority as a nation,
as a government, has to be defense. That's

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:36.470
more important than anything else. If you
can build nice highways, that's fine. You've

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:40.010
got infrastructure you've got to worry about.
We've got a lot of things the government does

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:44.410
and that we want to have done, but nothing's
more important than the safety and security

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:47.309
of the American people. And right now that's
being short-changed.

00:24:47.309 --> 00:24:50.669
Dany: There's no substitute for leadership.

00:24:50.669 --> 00:24:51.760
Cheney: Exactly right.

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:53.960
Dany: It's always such a pleasure. Thank you
very much.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.420
Cheney: Thanks, Dany. Enjoyed it.

